      "say": "As an app grows, you need ways to share behavior between components. React has a handful of patterns for this: composition, render props, higher-order components, and compound components.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-15-patterns/why.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "renderprops",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 11.0,
      "text": "Render props.",
      "visual": "code",
      "items": [
        [
          "<MouseTracker>",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "  {({ x, y }) => (",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "    <p>Mouse at {x}, {y}</p>",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "  )}",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "</MouseTracker>",
          "yellow"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "A render prop is a function prop that a component calls to decide what to draw. The component owns the logic, like tracking the mouse, and the caller decides how to display the data.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-15-patterns/renderprops.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "hoc",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 12.5,
      "text": "Higher-order components.",
      "visual": "code",
      "items": [
        [
          "function withLogging(Component) {",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "  return function Wrapped(props) {",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "    useEffect(() => log('shown'), []);",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "    return <Component {...props} />;",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "  };",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "}",
          "yellow"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "A higher-order component is a function that takes a component, and returns a new one with extra behavior, like logging. It was popular before hooks, and still appears in older code and libraries.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-15-patterns/hoc.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "hooks",
      "min": 8,
